{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His early life involved captivity following the Battle of Inab where he spent ten years as a prisoner in Aleppo before gaining freedom through a ransom payment. This long imprisonment shaped his worldview and made him deeply skeptical of reckless military adventures against Muslim forces. He became known for his pragmatic approach to diplomacy and his willingness to negotiate with Saladin when other crusader leaders preferred aggression.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nThe period of his confinement in Aleppo defined much of his later political strategy because he witnessed firsthand the strength of Muslim unity under leaders like Nur ad-Din. Upon returning to Tripoli he focused on stabilizing his own county rather than seeking glory through unnecessary wars. His experience taught him that survival required careful calculation instead of blind faith in divine intervention during battle. This mindset often put him at odds with more zealous factions within the crusader states who viewed any negotiation with Muslims as treasonous or weak.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nRaymond acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance during certain periods of his reign. During this time he tried to prevent Guy of Lusignan from gaining too much power because he believed Guy lacked the wisdom needed to lead the kingdom safely. The internal conflicts between Raymond and the court faction supporting Guy weakened the overall defense of the realm and created dangerous divisions among the nobility. These disputes distracted the Christian forces while Saladin gathered his armies for a major invasion that would eventually change the region forever.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nHis advice to avoid direct confrontation with Saladin was ignored by King Guy leading to the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the crusader army was almost completely destroyed. Raymond managed to escape the encirclement but the defeat resulted in the loss of Jerusalem and most other crusader holdings within a short timeframe. Historians often debate whether his retreat from the battlefield was an act of cowardice or a strategic necessity given the hopeless situation facing his troops. Regardless of these criticisms his life story illustrates the complex challenges faced by Latin rulers in the Holy Land who struggled to maintain their positions against rising Islamic powers while dealing with intense internal rivalries that ultimately doomed their enterprise. His death shortly after the battle marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and the broader crusader movement in the Levant.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0001192164}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0017315828}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.013701114}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.023258867}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -9.298757e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -3.8147045e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -2.7418175e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.5033983e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0